<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Community News</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Reporting From the School!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Recognize the main ideas of longer conversational exchanges.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize the main ideas in school news reports about community events (e.g., 'The school is organizing a clean-up day in the park next Saturday.' 'The class will visit Casco Viejo to learn about Panama's history.').
Can respond to simple questions when classmates ask for information about community events (e.g., 'Where is the school fair?' 'It's at the sports field.' 'When are the community's carnivals?' 'They are next year starting on February 14.' 'Where is the Fiestas Patrias parade?' 'It's on Central Avenue.').
Can identify specific details in conversations about local traditions and places (e.g., 'Our community is famous for its traditional dances.' 'In Panama, people celebrate the Desfile de las Mil Polleras in Las Tablas with colorful dresses and parades.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of new vocabulary and key concepts for the lesson's listening focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing 3-4 large pictures of local community events (e.g., a small town festival, a school fair, a local parade, a community clean-up day). Students listen to the teacher's prompts and point to the pictures. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" or "Do you have this in your community?" to activate prior knowledge. Students listen and respond with single words or short phrases they know.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words related to the theme "Reporting From the School!" and "Our Community News" using flashcards or images. Words like: <i>community, reporter, news, festival, parade, mural, tradition, colorful, celebrate, describe</i>. The teacher pronounces each word clearly, models the pronunciation, and asks students to repeat. The teacher specifically models the pronunciation of past tense -ed endings (e.g., `celebrated` /ɪd/, `liked` /t/, `described` /d/) and identifies short/long vowel sounds (e.g., /æ/ in `banner`, /eɪ/ in `parade`). The teacher also demonstrates how to form a simple past tense sentence like "Last year, our community celebrated a big festival."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary by asking questions and showing pictures or performing actions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'festival' a sad event or a happy celebration?" (Expected answer: "Happy celebration.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I am a 'reporter', do I tell stories or draw pictures?" (Expected answer: "Tell stories.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a 'parade'?" (Points to a picture of people marching with music). (Expected answer: "Yes, it is.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Community News" through an authentic listening text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio recording (approx. 1.5 - 2 minutes) of a school news report or an interview between two students (a reporter and a student) about a recent community event. The audio uses the target vocabulary (community, reporter, news, festival, parade, mural, tradition, colorful, celebrate, describe) and simple past tense structures (e.g., "We visited the school fair last weekend. We saw a colorful mural. People celebrated with music."). It also includes adjectives for physical appearance and potentially a compound sentence with 'but' (e.g., "I liked the music, but I didn't like the long line.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sharing past experiences and preferences: Can talk about past events and express likes or dislikes (e.g., 'I went to the Desfile de las Mil Polleras. I liked the different colorful polleras.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the audio once. The teacher asks, "What was the news report about?" or "What event did they talk about?" (e.g., "A school fair" or "A community festival"). Students listen and write down 1-2 main ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (True/False & Specific Details):</b> Students receive a worksheet with 4-5 simple True/False statements or matching questions related to the main ideas and some specific details from the report (e.g., "The school fair was on Saturday. True/False," "The reporter described a colorful mural. True/False," "They talked about a music festival. True/False"). Students listen a second time and mark their answers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Students practice recognizing and distinguishing specific language elements from the input.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for Past simple tense structures, target vocabulary (community, reporter, news, festival, parade, mural, tradition, colorful, celebrate, describe), compound sentences with 'but', and the pronunciation of -ed endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Complete:</b> The teacher plays segments of the audio again. Students have sentences with gaps from the news report and fill them in as they listen. For example: "The students _________ a mural about the community's _________." (prepared, tradition). This reinforces target vocabulary and past tense verbs. Teacher pauses after each segment and checks answers with the class, focusing on correct past tense forms.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Listen and Match the Feeling:</b> The teacher plays short phrases from the audio (e.g., "I liked the colorful floats," "I didn't like the loud music," "They celebrated with proud smiles"). Students listen and match each phrase to an emoji or a word describing a feeling (e.g., happy, sad, excited). This helps them connect spoken words to meaning and understand nuances, especially with sentences using 'but'. Teacher also highlights the pronunciation of '-ed' endings in verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check: Listen and Repeat the Detail:</b> In pairs, students receive a short list of simple questions about the news report (e.g., "What did the reporter describe?", "When did they celebrate?"). The teacher plays the audio one more time. Students listen for the answers. After listening, they ask each other the questions and listen to their partner's answers, checking for accuracy based on the audio. For example, Student A: "What did they describe?" Student B: "They described a colorful mural."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Community News." Focus on demonstrating listening comprehension and simple responses.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided mini-interview and Q&A. Students will role-play being a "school reporter" and a "student participant" from the community event mentioned in the audio. The focus is on the "student participant" listening to simple questions and responding with main ideas and details heard in the original news report, practicing past tense and target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Role Assignment:</b> Students work in pairs. One student is the "Reporter" and the other is the "Participant."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Reporter's Questions:</b> The teacher provides the "Reporters" with 3-4 simple questions based on the audio news report, using question words (who, what, where, when, why, how) and past simple tense (e.g., "What did you see at the festival?", "Where was the parade?", "Did you like the music or the food?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Participant's Listening and Response:</b> The "Participant" listens carefully to the "Reporter's" questions. They then respond using the main ideas, specific details, and vocabulary they recognized from the audio news report. They should try to use past simple tense and the conjunction 'but' if expressing a preference (e.g., "I saw a colorful mural. I liked the music, but I didn't like the loud sounds.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Peer Interaction:</b> Pairs practice their mini-interviews. The "Reporter" listens to the "Participant's" answers, ensuring they relate to the news report's content. The "Participant" focuses on clear listening and accurate recall of main ideas and details.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>5. Sharing:</b> A few pairs share their short interviews with the class. The class listens and identifies if the "Participant" correctly identified the main ideas and details from the original news report.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to evaluate students' listening comprehension of main ideas and their ability to recall details from a conversational exchange.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the mini-interviews and a short listening comprehension quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listening Comprehension Quiz:</b> Students listen to a slightly modified short version (30-45 seconds) of the original news report, focusing on main ideas. They then answer 2-3 multiple-choice questions or fill-in-the-blank questions based on the main ideas and key details.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Oral Response Check (from Stage 4):</b> During the mini-interviews, the teacher observes students' ability to listen to questions and respond with relevant information from the audio, using target vocabulary and past simple tense.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes the main ideas in the news report.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies specific details when asked simple questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (community, reporter, news, festival, parade, mural, tradition, colorful, celebrate, describe) intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses past simple tense correctly in responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces past tense -ed endings with reasonable accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand the main idea of a school news report about events in my community: (Circle one) Yes / Almost / Not yet"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned today is: __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future lessons based on student performance and engagement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the listening activity was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the listening activity was difficult? What can I do to improve my listening next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students and developing their listening skills? (e.g., visuals, varied audio, clear instructions)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to recognize main ideas or specific details? (e.g., pre-teaching more vocabulary, providing a transcript for a third listening, more repetition, slower audio)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students grasp the initial set of vocabulary and past tense structures?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter a short written text about a school or community event, utilizing the same core vocabulary (community, reporter, news, festival, parade, mural, tradition, colorful, celebrate, describe) and grammatical structures (past simple, compound sentences with 'but') introduced and practiced in this listening lesson. This will reinforce their understanding of the vocabulary in a new context (reading) and build upon the theme of "Our Community News" by introducing a written report.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>